      <h2>Two routes that should not be conflated</h2>
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Unfair dismissal</th><th>General protections</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>Core question</th><td>Was the dismissal harsh, unjust or unreasonable?</td><td>Was action taken for a prohibited reason?</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Typical forum</th><td>Fair Work Commission</td><td>Commission gateway for dismissal claims, then court unless resolved or arbitrated by consent; direct court route for many non-dismissal claims</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Evidence focus</th><td>Reason, process, proportionality and remedy</td><td>Protected matter, adverse action, decision-maker reasons and the reverse onus</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Start with</th><td><a href="/modules/who-can-claim">Module 1</a>, then <a href="/modules/valid-reason">Module 6</a></td><td><a href="/modules/general-protections">Module 2</a>, then the <a href="/modules/what-is-a-workplace-right">Modules 25–33 in depth</a></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h2>Use the map without turning it into a checklist</h2>
      <p>The point is not to complete every box. It is to avoid treating an issue as a simple conduct or performance matter when the facts may engage another source or protection.</p>
      <ul class="compact"><li>Identify the obvious pathway.</li><li>Ask what fact would activate a second pathway.</li><li>Check the source that governs that pathway.</li><li>Keep unresolved pathways visible while the facts are tested.</li></ul>
      <p class="backlink"><a href="/start-here">← Start here</a> · <a href="/">Return to the guide</a></p>
